4D Quantum State Simulation with Cirq and Streamlit
This project demonstrates the representation and manipulation of 4D quantum states using a two-qubit quantum system. Built with Streamlit for interactivity, Cirq for quantum circuit simulation, and Matplotlib for visualization, this application explores quantum concepts like superposition, entanglement, and higher-dimensional state manipulations.

Features
Quantum Circuit Creation:

Construct and visualize a quantum circuit representing a 4D state space using two qubits.
Features include superposition (via Hadamard gates), entanglement (via CNOT gates), and rotations in different dimensions (via rx and ry gates).
Interactive Parameters:

Users can adjust rotation angles (θ1 and θ2) via Streamlit sliders to observe their impact on the quantum state.
State Vector Visualization:

Displays the probabilities of each basis state (|00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩).
Visualizes the real part of the density matrix to illustrate quantum correlations.
Measurement Statistics:

Simulates the circuit multiple times to generate measurement statistics.
Displays results as a histogram showing the frequency of each basis state.
Detailed Explanation:

Explains how the quantum circuit represents a 4D state and explores concepts like superposition, entanglement, and dimensional rotations.
Installation
Prerequisites
Python 3.8 or later
pip package manager
Required Libraries
Install the required dependencies using pip:

bash
Copy code
pip install streamlit cirq numpy matplotlib
Running the Application
Clone or download the repository.
Navigate to the project directory.
Run the Streamlit app:
bash
Copy code
streamlit run app.py
Open the provided local URL in your web browser to view the application.
Application Overview
Circuit Representation
The quantum circuit uses two qubits (q0 and q1) to represent a 4-dimensional Hilbert space.
Components:
Hadamard Gates: Create superpositions across all basis states.
CNOT Gate: Introduces entanglement between qubits.
Rotation Gates (rx and ry): Apply rotations to explore different planes of the 4D state space.
Visualization
State Probabilities:
A bar chart displays the probabilities of each basis state after the circuit simulation.
Density Matrix:
Visualizes the real part of the density matrix, highlighting quantum correlations.
Measurement Statistics
Simulates the circuit 1000 times and displays a histogram of measurement outcomes.
Shows the frequency of each basis state (|00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩).
Parameters
Adjustable Inputs
θ1: Controls the rotation angle for the rx gate applied to the first qubit.
θ2: Controls the rotation angle for the ry gate applied to the second qubit.
Output Displays
Quantum circuit diagram.
State probabilities.
Real part of the density matrix.
Measurement statistics histogram.
Example Output
Quantum Circuit:

css
Copy code
0: ───H───@───Rx(θ1)───
          │
1: ───H───X───Ry(θ2)───
State Probabilities:

Probability of each basis state displayed as a bar chart.
Density Matrix:

A heatmap representing the real part of the density matrix.
Measurement Statistics:

Histogram of the frequency of each basis state after repeated measurements.
Key Quantum Concepts
Superposition:

The ability of quantum bits to exist in multiple states simultaneously.
Achieved using Hadamard gates.
Entanglement:

A quantum phenomenon where qubits become interconnected, such that the state of one qubit depends on the state of the other.
Created using a CNOT gate.
Rotations in Higher Dimensions:

The rx and ry gates apply rotations to explore the higher-dimensional state space.
Troubleshooting
Simulation Errors:

If the simulation fails, ensure that all dependencies are correctly installed and try adjusting parameters.
Errors are displayed in the Streamlit app interface.
Display Issues:

Refresh the browser if visualizations do not appear as expected.
Future Enhancements
Extend to higher qubit systems for 8D, 16D, etc., simulations.
Include support for quantum gates that operate on multiple qubits simultaneously (e.g., Toffoli or Fredkin gates).
Add 3D visualizations of state vectors using libraries like Plotly.
